WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:00.763
Welcome.

2
00:00:00.763 --> 00:00:05.608
This briefing comes from Book 17 of
the Federal Modernization Workbook,

3
00:00:05.608 --> 00:00:09.000
AI Identity Governance, the
last book in the series.

4
00:00:09.000 --> 00:00:12.662
It's written for Chief AI
Officers, CIOs and CISOs,

5
00:00:12.662 --> 00:00:18.338
identity and security operations teams,
ISSOs, and authorizing officials.

6
00:00:18.338 --> 00:00:19.750
I'll take four questions.

7
00:00:19.750 --> 00:00:22.712
Why do AI agents need
identities and owners?

8
00:00:22.712 --> 00:00:24.412
What does governing them involve?

9
00:00:24.412 --> 00:00:26.113
Who owns AI identity?

10
00:00:26.113 --> 00:00:30.626
And how do you scan the inventory and
evidence AI systems for authorization?

11
00:00:30.626 --> 00:00:35.175
The short version: an AI system that
can act is an account with permissions.

12
00:00:35.175 --> 00:00:38.173
Most were deployed outside
the identity program,

13
00:00:38.173 --> 00:00:41.537
and there's no separate AI
authorization to fall back on.

14
00:00:41.537 --> 00:00:44.270
Let's start with what
makes an agent an account.

15
00:00:48.020 --> 00:00:50.920
A chat assistant that
drafts a memo is a tool.

16
00:00:50.920 --> 00:00:55.345
Nothing it does reaches a record until
a person reads the draft and acts.

17
00:00:55.345 --> 00:00:56.783
An agent is different.

18
00:00:56.783 --> 00:01:01.819
It sends replies from a mailbox,
closes tickets, updates case records.

19
00:01:01.819 --> 00:01:04.519
Each of those is an action
against agency data,

20
00:01:04.519 --> 00:01:07.857
taken by something that signed
in and holds permissions.

21
00:01:07.857 --> 00:01:09.369
That makes it an identity.

22
00:01:09.369 --> 00:01:14.536
An AI system can reach data in one
of four ways: for a signed-in user,

23
00:01:14.536 --> 00:01:18.532
as itself, as its own user
account, or with an API key.

24
00:01:18.532 --> 00:01:21.582
The first three run through
the identity provider.

25
00:01:21.582 --> 00:01:26.045
The fourth skips it entirely, so
no sign-in is ever evaluated.

26
00:01:26.045 --> 00:01:29.068
Today the agentic share
of federal AI is small,

27
00:01:29.068 --> 00:01:33.045
only a small fraction of the
thousands of reported use cases.

28
00:01:33.045 --> 00:01:35.208
But it's the share growing fastest.

29
00:01:35.208 --> 00:01:36.970
So how did AI get in?

30
00:01:40.720 --> 00:01:44.520
People join through a pipeline
the identity program built,

31
00:01:44.520 --> 00:01:47.545
from an HR record all the
way to a leaver process.

32
00:01:47.545 --> 00:01:49.395
AI mostly came in another way.

33
00:01:49.395 --> 00:01:51.908
A mission office ran
a pilot with a vendor.

34
00:01:51.908 --> 00:01:55.183
A developer put an API
key in a config file.

35
00:01:55.183 --> 00:01:58.957
A product the agency already
licensed shipped an assistant.

36
00:01:58.957 --> 00:02:01.474
None of those paths produces an owner,

37
00:02:01.474 --> 00:02:04.721
a placement, a governed
credential or an end date.

38
00:02:04.721 --> 00:02:06.858
And the risk is identity-shaped.

39
00:02:06.858 --> 00:02:11.345
Take prompt injection, where planted
input makes a system misbehave.

40
00:02:11.345 --> 00:02:16.200
Against an agent with broad permissions,
a successful injection is an action,

41
00:02:16.200 --> 00:02:20.145
a message sent or a record changed,
under a legitimate identity.

42
00:02:20.145 --> 00:02:23.232
The blast radius is
whatever access it holds.

43
00:02:23.232 --> 00:02:26.344
The good news is that no new
control family is needed.

44
00:02:26.344 --> 00:02:29.517
The gap is coverage: the AI
system's account is often

45
00:02:29.517 --> 00:02:33.790
missing from the inventory those
controls are tested against.

46
00:02:37.540 --> 00:02:40.953
Federal AI policy was rewritten in 2025.

47
00:02:40.953 --> 00:02:44.661
One OMB memo governs use,
with a Chief AI Officer,

48
00:02:44.661 --> 00:02:50.177
an annual use-case inventory, and
minimum practices for high-impact AI.

49
00:02:50.177 --> 00:02:54.706
Its companion governs acquisition,
and says AI run as an information

50
00:02:54.706 --> 00:02:58.678
system needs an authorization
to operate before it deploys.

51
00:02:58.678 --> 00:03:01.316
What the rules don't do
matters just as much.

52
00:03:01.316 --> 00:03:03.765
There's no AI-specific authorization.

53
00:03:03.765 --> 00:03:08.459
Agencies keep following their existing
authorization process, the memo's own

54
00:03:08.459 --> 00:03:13.152
risk acceptance doesn't replace it, and
it sets no identity requirements at all.

55
00:03:13.152 --> 00:03:16.883
So a high-impact use case needs
two decisions before it deploys:

56
00:03:16.883 --> 00:03:21.903
the signed risk acceptance on its impact
assessment, and the system's authorization.

57
00:03:21.903 --> 00:03:27.391
One footnote: a recent executive order
changes the preferred term in some documents,

58
00:03:27.391 --> 00:03:32.016
but Book 17 keeps saying AI, because
the instruments it applies still do.

59
00:03:32.016 --> 00:03:34.090
Next, the inventory.

60
00:03:37.840 --> 00:03:42.455
The use-case inventory is the only
government-wide list of federal AI,

61
00:03:42.455 --> 00:03:45.127
so the program reads it as identity data.

62
00:03:45.127 --> 00:03:47.215
A few fields are solid anchors.

63
00:03:47.215 --> 00:03:51.690
The use-case ID is stable and never
reused, so it can key a record.

64
00:03:51.690 --> 00:03:53.353
The bureau gives a placement.

65
00:03:53.353 --> 00:03:56.214
And a stage of Retired is a leaver event.

66
00:03:56.214 --> 00:04:01.553
Other fields are only leads; the contact,
for example, may be a public inbox.

67
00:04:01.553 --> 00:04:06.345
But the facts that decide access aren't
there at all: what the system signs in as,

68
00:04:06.345 --> 00:04:10.089
its credential, its permissions,
and the person accountable.

69
00:04:10.089 --> 00:04:14.269
The high-impact fields report status,
not evidence, and in the published

70
00:04:14.269 --> 00:04:18.590
file a large share of deployed high-impact
use cases answered none of them.

71
00:04:18.590 --> 00:04:20.551
And a use case isn't an identity,

72
00:04:20.551 --> 00:04:24.002
so the program records the
link on the directory side.

73
00:04:24.002 --> 00:04:28.080
Next, how to tell what kind
of identity an AI system is.

74
00:04:31.830 --> 00:04:35.447
Here's the key idea: an AI
system's kind of identity is

75
00:04:35.447 --> 00:04:39.580
decided by whose token it
presents, not by the model it runs.

76
00:04:39.580 --> 00:04:42.967
Acting for a signed-in user,
the user's policies apply,

77
00:04:42.967 --> 00:04:46.117
and the agent can reach
everything that user can.

78
00:04:46.117 --> 00:04:50.518
Acting as itself, the only Conditional
Access option is to block it.

79
00:04:50.518 --> 00:04:55.505
Acting as its own user account, it
slips past policies aimed at all users.

80
00:04:55.505 --> 00:05:00.230
And with a stored API key,
Entra ID issues no token at all.

81
00:05:00.230 --> 00:05:04.180
Microsoft Entra Agent ID adds
identity types built for agents,

82
00:05:04.180 --> 00:05:09.193
including a blueprint that holds the
credentials and a required human sponsor.

83
00:05:09.193 --> 00:05:11.456
But it only governs agents that use it.

84
00:05:11.456 --> 00:05:16.023
Most AI still runs as ordinary service
principals and managed identities,

85
00:05:16.023 --> 00:05:18.455
and the same governance applies to them.

86
00:05:18.455 --> 00:05:21.620
Next, what's actually
available, and where.

87
00:05:25.370 --> 00:05:28.544
The agency's cloud
decides what it can use,

88
00:05:28.544 --> 00:05:32.932
so Book 17 dates every product
statement to October 2026.

89
00:05:32.932 --> 00:05:38.416
The core Agent ID platform, with
blueprints, agent identities and sponsors,

90
00:05:38.416 --> 00:05:42.219
was announced as generally
available in April 2026.

91
00:05:42.219 --> 00:05:44.766
But several controls are still preview,

92
00:05:44.766 --> 00:05:48.007
including agent targeting
in Conditional Access.

93
00:05:48.007 --> 00:05:49.296
The slide lists them.

94
00:05:49.296 --> 00:05:51.507
The government clouds
are the bigger issue.

95
00:05:51.507 --> 00:05:57.121
GCC has a partial, documented subset:
identity and access governance for agents,

96
00:05:57.121 --> 00:06:01.883
but not Microsoft's audit, data loss
prevention or network controls.

97
00:06:01.883 --> 00:06:05.154
GCC High and DOD have no
published availability,

98
00:06:05.154 --> 00:06:09.570
so treat agent identities as
unavailable there until Microsoft

99
00:06:09.570 --> 00:06:14.232
confirms otherwise in writing, and
govern AI as workload identities.

100
00:06:14.232 --> 00:06:18.524
The rule is simple: build on what's
generally available in your own cloud,

101
00:06:18.524 --> 00:06:20.996
and keep preview features as watch items.

102
00:06:20.996 --> 00:06:23.840
Next, the person behind each agent.

103
00:06:27.590 --> 00:06:30.686
An agent has no hire date
and no separation date,

104
00:06:30.686 --> 00:06:33.940
so its lifecycle has to hang
from a person who does.

105
00:06:33.940 --> 00:06:37.438
Agent ID separates the owner,
who manages credentials,

106
00:06:37.438 --> 00:06:41.403
from the sponsor, who's accountable
for purpose and lifecycle.

107
00:06:41.403 --> 00:06:45.253
Left to defaults, the sponsor is
just whoever built the agent.

108
00:06:45.253 --> 00:06:50.206
The program decides instead: the sponsor
is a federal employee mastered in HR, a

109
00:06:50.206 --> 00:06:55.389
small group of stewards is the backup, and
the technical owner is never the sponsor.

110
00:06:55.389 --> 00:06:57.602
The lifecycle then follows that person.

111
00:06:57.602 --> 00:07:01.716
When the sponsor moves or leaves,
the HR event triggers a transfer,

112
00:07:01.716 --> 00:07:06.364
and the new sponsor has a short window
to confirm the agent is still needed.

113
00:07:06.364 --> 00:07:10.340
Access comes with end dates, so a
sponsor who does nothing removes it.

114
00:07:10.340 --> 00:07:15.028
And an orphaned agent is disabled by
a person, never by an automatic rule.

115
00:07:15.028 --> 00:07:17.780
Next, credentials and privilege.

116
00:07:21.530 --> 00:07:24.181
An agent can't be asked
for a second factor,

117
00:07:24.181 --> 00:07:27.455
so everything it may do has
to be decided in advance.

118
00:07:27.455 --> 00:07:28.893
Start with the credential.

119
00:07:28.893 --> 00:07:33.743
In Agent ID it lives on the blueprint and
is shared by every agent made from it,

120
00:07:33.743 --> 00:07:36.592
so the blueprint is the
credential boundary.

121
00:07:36.592 --> 00:07:41.711
The order of preference is settled: federated
credentials first, a certificate in a

122
00:07:41.711 --> 00:07:46.681
vault only where that won't work, and a
client secret only for development and test.

123
00:07:46.681 --> 00:07:47.855
Then privilege.

124
00:07:47.855 --> 00:07:51.032
Microsoft blocks agents
from Global Administrator,

125
00:07:51.032 --> 00:07:53.993
but still allows some
other powerful admin roles.

126
00:07:53.993 --> 00:07:56.197
That's a floor, not least privilege,

127
00:07:56.197 --> 00:08:01.155
so the program's rule is that no AI
identity holds a standing privileged role.

128
00:08:01.155 --> 00:08:04.900
And in GCC, where Purview's
agent controls aren't available,

129
00:08:04.900 --> 00:08:07.630
scoped permissions are the data boundary.

130
00:08:07.630 --> 00:08:09.880
Next, watching for drift.

131
00:08:13.630 --> 00:08:16.605
Every AI identity drifts once it exists.

132
00:08:16.605 --> 00:08:21.842
A sponsor changes jobs, a developer adds
a secret, an agent gets a new tool.

133
00:08:21.842 --> 00:08:26.907
Federal policy asks that high-impact AI be
monitored for changes after deployment,

134
00:08:26.907 --> 00:08:30.504
and the identity logs are where
those changes show up first.

135
00:08:30.504 --> 00:08:35.888
So the program collects every AI identity's
sign-ins, audit events and risk signals,

136
00:08:35.888 --> 00:08:38.501
and ties each one to its identity record,

137
00:08:38.501 --> 00:08:42.380
because nothing in a service
principal's sign-in says it's AI.

138
00:08:42.380 --> 00:08:44.805
Discovery is a standing input too.

139
00:08:44.805 --> 00:08:49.063
Shadow AI turns up in several
places, from services people use to

140
00:08:49.063 --> 00:08:53.643
agents on endpoints, and anything
found without a record is a finding.

141
00:08:53.643 --> 00:08:56.590
An agentic system with
no governed identity

142
00:08:56.590 --> 00:08:59.379
is the finding this
book exists to prevent.

143
00:08:59.379 --> 00:09:03.968
It halts, alerts a named person,
and is never fixed automatically.

144
00:09:03.968 --> 00:09:06.906
And API keys stay invisible to all of it.

145
00:09:06.906 --> 00:09:09.130
So who owns these decisions?

146
00:09:12.880 --> 00:09:17.480
Ask who owns an AI agent's identity
and three offices answer at once.

147
00:09:17.480 --> 00:09:21.868
The way out is to separate the decisions
and give each one a single decider.

148
00:09:21.868 --> 00:09:25.517
AI adds one new executive
and one new kind of decision.

149
00:09:25.517 --> 00:09:31.243
The Chief AI Officer owns the inventory,
the high-impact determinations and waivers.

150
00:09:31.243 --> 00:09:35.593
The official accountable for the mission
signs the use-case risk acceptance.

151
00:09:35.593 --> 00:09:37.882
Neither one authorizes the system;

152
00:09:37.882 --> 00:09:42.542
the authorizing official alone
accepts its security and privacy risk.

153
00:09:42.542 --> 00:09:46.217
For the identity itself, the
sponsor, an agency employee,

154
00:09:46.217 --> 00:09:48.854
decides whether the agent should exist.

155
00:09:48.854 --> 00:09:52.618
The technical owner, who may be
a contractor, keeps it running.

156
00:09:52.618 --> 00:09:57.493
And the identity governance officer
decides consent, transfers and orphans.

157
00:09:57.493 --> 00:10:01.793
Two signatures stay two, even
when one person holds both roles.

158
00:10:01.793 --> 00:10:04.130
Now, the inventory scan.

159
00:10:07.880 --> 00:10:12.333
The inventory scan is the program's
own method, not a federal requirement,

160
00:10:12.333 --> 00:10:16.118
and it's cheap because it reads
answers agencies already gave.

161
00:10:16.118 --> 00:10:20.051
Load the file, detect the gaps,
put agentic systems first,

162
00:10:20.051 --> 00:10:25.267
route each finding to a named role, and
close it only on a later observation.

163
00:10:25.267 --> 00:10:26.580
The scale is real.

164
00:10:26.580 --> 00:10:31.376
In the published 2025 file, most
piloted or deployed use cases trip at

165
00:10:31.376 --> 00:10:36.592
least one check, often a missing
authorization answer or a missing contact.

166
00:10:36.592 --> 00:10:40.730
Those are answers to reconcile,
not findings against agencies.

167
00:10:40.730 --> 00:10:45.318
The checks that matter most need the
directory, so only the agency can run them.

168
00:10:45.318 --> 00:10:46.904
Is there an identity record?

169
00:10:46.904 --> 00:10:48.355
An accountable sponsor?

170
00:10:48.355 --> 00:10:52.005
A retired use case whose
identities still hold access?

171
00:10:52.005 --> 00:10:55.728
A central scan of the public file
could start that conversation,

172
00:10:55.728 --> 00:10:57.343
but it can't see a credential.

173
00:10:57.343 --> 00:10:59.470
Finally, the evidence.

174
00:11:03.220 --> 00:11:06.183
Everything ends in the
authorization package,

175
00:11:06.183 --> 00:11:11.044
and it has to answer five questions about
each AI system's identity: who owns it,

176
00:11:11.044 --> 00:11:13.475
how it authenticates, what it can reach,

177
00:11:13.475 --> 00:11:16.058
who's watching it, and
when its access ends.

178
00:11:16.058 --> 00:11:19.269
Each answer has an artifact
and controls behind it.

179
00:11:19.269 --> 00:11:20.620
Two points stand out.

180
00:11:20.620 --> 00:11:26.201
First, the control for service identification
and authentication describes exactly

181
00:11:26.201 --> 00:11:29.302
an AI system calling a tool
with its own identity,

182
00:11:29.302 --> 00:11:34.108
yet it's in no NIST baseline and no FedRAMP
class, so the program tailors it in.

183
00:11:34.108 --> 00:11:39.145
Second, the identity program can evidence
three of the high-impact practices,

184
00:11:39.145 --> 00:11:42.608
for example a fail-safe that's
simply a tested disable.

185
00:11:42.608 --> 00:11:47.321
The package can be machine-readable in
OSCAL, with nothing AI-specific needed.

186
00:11:47.321 --> 00:11:49.716
NIST's AI overlays aren't drafted yet,

187
00:11:49.716 --> 00:11:53.470
so tailor the baseline now and
compare when they're final.

188
00:11:53.470 --> 00:11:55.690
That brings us to the decisions.

189
00:11:59.440 --> 00:12:00.815
So, four decisions.

190
00:12:00.815 --> 00:12:06.682
Count AI identities, not AI projects: no
use case is recorded as piloted or deployed

191
00:12:06.682 --> 00:12:12.290
without a link to its identity record,
created before any credential is issued.

192
00:12:12.290 --> 00:12:16.477
Put a person's name on every one,
an agency employee as sponsor,

193
00:12:16.477 --> 00:12:20.077
with HR's mover and leaver
events reaching the agent.

194
00:12:20.077 --> 00:12:23.733
Take away stored secrets and
standing privilege, and keep the

195
00:12:23.733 --> 00:12:28.678
block-unless-approved policy in report-only
mode while agent targeting is preview.

196
00:12:28.678 --> 00:12:33.177
And authorize AI through the process
you already run, with two signatures

197
00:12:33.177 --> 00:12:37.528
for high-impact AI and the service
authentication control tailored in.

198
00:12:37.528 --> 00:12:39.678
The next step needs no new tooling.

199
00:12:39.678 --> 00:12:43.152
Run the inventory checks on
the agency's own submission,

200
00:12:43.152 --> 00:12:46.702
start with agentic systems
and missing authorizations,

201
00:12:46.702 --> 00:12:50.328
and confirm each agent capability
in your own cloud first.

202
00:12:50.328 --> 00:12:53.778
Every AI system needs an
identity and an owner.

203
00:12:53.778 --> 00:12:57.728
Book 17 has the detail,
every claim footnoted.

204
00:12:57.728 --> 00:12:58.550
Thank you.
